  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Entrepreneurship development policies and programmes have been implemented for the creation of productive employment and decent work</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Within the context of Trinidad and Tobago&#x2019;s national development plan, &quot;Vision 2040,&quot; the government aims to build globally competitive businesses by creating a conducive business environment through proper training, education, and strengthening institutions involved in enterprise development. The COVID-19 pandemic significantly impacted private and public sector activities, particularly affecting the MSME sector, which is characterized by low productivity and high informality. This sector is crucial for the income of many families, especially in low-income areas like East Port of Spain.

To address these challenges, the ILO plans to collaborate with key stakeholders who support vulnerable youth as Ministry of Youth Development and National Service and Ministry of Labour. Government in realizing a survey to have more adequate data on youth in formal and informal business to feed into more adequate youth development policies. This collaboration will also involve transferring knowledge of the ILO&apos;s Start and Improve Your Business (SIYB) methodologies to local trainers, supported by an international certified Master Trainer. . The ILO will also provide technical assistance to the National Productivity Council to address productivity issues in the region.</narrative>
  </description>
